{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem he played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century \n*Early Life* \nhe was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father was killed by the Hashshashin he was still a minor at the time and the regency was exercised by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem \nhe grew up in the court of Jerusalem and was educated in the ways of warfare and statecraft he became a close friend and advisor to King Amalric I of Jerusalem who succeeded Baldwin III \n*Reign* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli played a significant role in the wars against the Muslim states of Syria and Egypt he was a skilled military leader and led several successful campaigns against the enemies of the Kingdom of Jerusalem \nhe was also a skilled diplomat and negotiated several treaties with the Muslim states including the Treaty of Aleppo which established a temporary peace between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Zengid dynasty \n*Marriage and Children* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli married Hodierna of Jerusalem but the marriage was childless he had several illegitimate children including a son named Raymond who died young \nhe also had a close relationship with his niece Sibylla of Jerusalem who later became the Queen of Jerusalem \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1187 while on a campaign against the Zengid dynasty he was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his godson Raymond of Antioch \nhe was remembered as a wise and courageous leader who played a significant role in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli left a lasting legacy in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli he was a skilled military leader and diplomat who played a significant role in the wars against the Muslim states of Syria and Egypt \nhe was also a wise and just ruler who was loved by his people and respected by his enemies \n*Capture and Imprisonment* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was captured by the Zengid dynasty in 1164 and held prisoner for several years he was eventually released and returned to Tripoli where he continued to rule as Count \nhe never forgot his experiences as a prisoner and worked tirelessly to improve the defenses of the County of Tripoli and to protect its people from the threats of the Muslim states \n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem* \nRaymond III Count of Tripoli had a close relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and worked closely with its rulers to defend the kingdom against the threats of the Muslim states \nhe was a skilled diplomat and negotiated several treaties with the Muslim states including the Treaty of Aleppo which established a temporary peace between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Zengid dynasty", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0009117737063206732}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0004573014739435166}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.4371652603149414}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.6498938202857971}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.536697689327411e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -5.602820692729438e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.1148839071393013}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -9.298280929215252e-06}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.002186647616326809}, {"token": " ", "l